  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(X∨Y)⇒Y≡¬(¬(X∨Y))∨Y.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(X∨Y))∨Y≡(X∨Y)∨Y.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the associative and idempotent law, we have\n\n$$\n(X∨Y)∨Y≡X∨Y.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $X∨Y.$",
